A||||4||Joe Torma||Minor oversight on my part...||||1||
Z||000000||Joe Torma||12-30-2001||08:31 PM||||Ok, dopey me just remembered that my car is filled with water only.  No, I haven't tried to start the car and am fully aware that the block will crack if I do.<P>But, is anything I should be weary of after its stay in the gararge thawing out?<P>I don't think anything is going to happen to the engine, maybe the hoses though.  What do you guys think?<P>------------------<BR>Its never going to be finished...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||12-30-2001||10:01 PM||||After it thaws, check the oil. If it has water in it, someting serious has cracked. If it looks okay, start it up and run it for a while. Turn it off and check the oil again. If it looks like light coffee, see above. <BR>This would frighten the hell out of me. I saw a V-8 Chevy block get cracked by freezing. Hopefully, it didn't sit long enough to freeze solid.<BR>Good luck. <BR>Put some anti-freeze in as soon as you can.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||12-30-2001||10:25 PM||||Joe,It hasn't been that cold.Even if the temperature dips into the 20,s overnight you should be ok.The daytime temps have been above freezing.Just drain it and refill with 50/50 mix of antifreeze.Don't forget to have your heater on high(temp)to circulate the mix through your heater core. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Joe Torma||12-30-2001||11:30 PM||||Yup Joe's a moron.<P>Well, one possible saving grace is that I must have accidentally popped off a small water line(when I don't know--probably 2 weeks ago since I haven't touched the car recently) that is at the bottom of the block, by the oil filter.<P>This was for the oil cooler I didn't install yet.  I jacked it up and theres a big ol puddle of water under there.  Probably most of what's supposed to be in the block.<P>The car's in an instant garage that heats up a bit during the day.<P>Thanks for the little reassurance Hugh...and Raydar for scaring the poop put of me.<P>I hope it'll be ok.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-31-2001||12:01 AM||||Ice is hard to explain..... in short it depends just how hard it froze. There's a bunch of differant levels of froze besides the obvious ice cube.<P>Hoses and thing freeze faster than the block and head by some margine depending on the temperatures involved. If you popped a hose, you may also have popped freeze plugs, radiator tubes and other small things.(Obviously you may also have done much worse.)<P>Unfortunatly there is no way to know untill it thaws.<P>Get 1-2 of those little ceramic heaters and put in there with it. (I don't know exactly where you're storing it... Don't set them directly on or near thin plastics. Be very careful with the heaters.) You want to get that water and ice out ASAP.<P>If it froze in the water pump, and you've tried running it, you likely snapped the impeller off the shaft. Very easy to do with plastic pumps but can happen with metal to.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
